Given: X = {1 ≤ n ≤ 8}

P = 'n is an even integer' → P = {2, 4, 6, 8}
Q = '(3 ≤ n ≤ 7) ∧ (n ≠ 6)' → Q = {3, 4, 5, 7}

For P ⇔ Q (biconditional), both propositions must have same truth value.

- n = 1: P=false, Q=false → true
- n = 2: P=true, Q=false → false
- n = 3: P=false, Q=true → false
- n = 4: P=true, Q=true → true
- n = 5: P=false, Q=true → false
- n = 6: P=true, Q=false → false
- n = 7: P=false, Q=true → false
- n = 8: P=true, Q=false → false

Truth set = {1, 4}

